// Searching a list of ints.
//
// Two functions rather than one, because two callers want two different things:
// `lst_index_of` answers *where* and `lst_find` answers *whether*. They are also
// two functions because `lst_find` delegating is what keeps them from being one
// duplicate-logic error -- a second linear scan with the same shape would be
// rejected however it was spelled.

// The first index holding v, or -1. A full scan every time: there is no break,
// so a hit near the front costs the same as a miss. For anything hot, keep a
// parallel index rather than calling this in a loop.
lst_index_of(int[] xs, int v) {
  int at = 0 - 1;
  int i = 0;
  while (i < len(xs)) {
    at = lst_pick(at, i, xs[i] = v);
    i = i + 1;
  }
} return int at;
([4, 9, 4], 4):(0)
([4, 9, 4], 7):(-1)

// 1 when v is in the list, 0 when it is not.
lst_find(int[] xs, int v) {
} return int lst_index_of(xs, v) >= 0;
([1, 2, 3], 2):(1)
([1, 2, 3], 5):(0)

// The last element. **Aborts on an empty list** -- an out-of-range list read is
// a process abort in this language, not a zero, so the caller checks len first.
lst_last(int[] xs) {
} return int xs[len(xs) - 1];
([1, 2, 3]):(3)
([9]):(9)
